Looking at the file, the scalar resolvers are internally inconsistent in one spot: the core-schema integer tag's **explicit** pattern is

```js
YAML_INTEGER_EXPLICIT_PATTERN$1 = "^(?:[-+]?0b[0-1]+|[-+]0o[0-7]+|[-+]?0x[0-9a-fA-F]+|[-+]?[0-9]+)$"
```

The octal alternative `[-+]0o[0-7]+` is missing the `?` after the sign class — unlike the JSON variant of the *same* pattern in this file (`[-+]?0o[0-7]+`), and unlike the core *implicit* pattern, which accepts unsigned octal (`0o[0-7]+`). The explicit pattern is supposed to be a superset of the implicit one (it adds binary and signed octal), so dropping `?` means unsigned octal resolves implicitly but fails when tagged explicitly.

Observable effect: under the default `CORE_SCHEMA`, plain `0o777` loads as `511`, but `load('!!int 0o777')` throws `cannot resolve a node with !<tag:yaml.org,2002:int> explicit tag`. Signed forms (`!!int -0o17`) still work, which is why the library's own suite can stay green while this path is broken. Everything else I checked (float/int/null/bool variant sets and first-char dispatch buckets, timestamp, block-scalar folding, dump quoting via `resolveImplicitScalarTag`, tag compilation/replacement in `withTags`) is self-consistent.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "test/core/proof.test.mjs", "content": "import { describe, it } from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert';
import { load, dump } from 'js-yaml';

describe('proof', () => {
  it('resolves an explicit !!int tag on unsigned octal', () => {
    // Implicit unsigned octal is accepted by the core int pattern...
    assert.strictEqual(load('0o777'), 511);
    // ...so an explicit !!int tag must accept it too. The core explicit int
    // pattern requires a sign on octal ([-+]0o instead of [-+]?0o), unlike
    // the JSON variant, so this throws on the patched build:
    assert.strictEqual(load('!!int 0o777'), 511);
    assert.strictEqual(load('!!int 0o0'), 0);
    assert.strictEqual(dump(load('!!int 0o777')), '511\n');
  });
});
"}, "note": "In YAML_INTEGER_EXPLICIT_PATTERN$1 (the core !!int tag) the octal alternative lost its '?': [-+]0o[0-7]+ requires a sign, while the implicit pattern and the JSON variant ([-+]?0o[0-7]+) accept unsigned octal. As a result load('!!int 0o777') throws instead of returning 511."}
```

